#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096390 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9283940a-cad8-51b5-b8c2-dc42ad8de264" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d03736b3-a7d8-52b3-894d-4ce2d04ee31c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14257" :question/problem "If $f(x) = 12 \\cdot 2^{x}$, then $f(-4)=$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate $f(-4)$, we substitute $x=-4$ into the expression for $f(x)$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-4) &= 12 \\cdot 2^{-4} \\\\\n&= 12 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2^{4}} \\\\\n&= 12 \\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{16} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096391 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096658 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6bab04f2-5e9a-55e7-9669-63e15938be0a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Function in a Modeling Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "a83c1301-18cf-5622-8a4a-73f9d18e36b8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14260" :question/problem "The formula $M(t)=128 \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^{t}$ gives the mass (in grams) of a radioactive calcium isotope after $t$ years.\nWhat mass of isotope remains after $3$ years?" :question/worked-solution "To get the mass of the isotope after $3$ years, we evaluate the function at $t=3$. Substituting $t=3$ into the function $M(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= 128(\\frac{1}{4})^{t} \\\\\n&= 128(\\frac{1}{4})^{3} \\\\\n&= 128 \\cdot \\frac{1^{3}}{4^{3}} \\\\\n&= 128 \\cdot \\frac{1}{64} \\\\\n&= \\frac{128}{64} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $3$ years, there will be $2$ grams of isotope remaining."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096392 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096659 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cee2b0-6cc8-5281-8ea2-02400b2980e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Initial Value"}] :question/id #uuid "ca8cd4d6-8136-59bb-a033-6ba9ae8c06ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-14262" :question/problem "Solve for $C$ given that $y(t) = C \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{t}$ and $y(5)=1$." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $t=5$ and $y(t)=1$ into the expression for $y(t)$, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= C \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{5} \\\\\n1 &= C \\cdot \\frac{1^{5}}{2^{5}} \\\\\n1 &= C \\cdot \\frac{1}{32} \\\\\n1 \\cdot 32 &= C \\\\\n32 &= C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C=32$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096393 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096660 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a21f047-5100-5f8b-9448-a5c2044e4e65" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Input Value"}] :question/id #uuid "02cbb721-a880-5600-8093-fc768ae5e993" :question/math-academy-id "e-14264" :question/problem "Given that $P(x) = 2 \\cdot 5^{3x}$, find $x$ such that $P(x)=250$." :question/worked-solution "First we substitute\n\n$$\nP(x)=250\n$$\n\ninto the equation:\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 5^{3x}= 250\n$$\n\nWe can divide both sides of the equation by the constant factor of $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2 \\cdot 5^{3x}}{2} &= \\frac{250}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{2 \\cdot 5^{3x}}{2} &= 125 \\\\\n5^{3x} &= 125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, using the fact that $125$ is a power of $5$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5^{3x} &= 5^{3} \\\\\n3x &= 3 \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096657 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9283940a-cad8-51b5-b8c2-dc42ad8de264" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "a730ddba-a0ca-5b47-bb1a-ec0ce7519cb0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095119}] :question/id #uuid "6b3ff04e-fd71-56bd-a3d9-91bf504b1369" :question/math-academy-id "q-218564" :question/problem "If $g(x) = 8 \\cdot (\\frac{3}{4})^{x}$, then $g(2) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
